|~ `::keyword` and what is declared under it: its constructor and its
   refusal, the comparisons of keywords, and `keyword`, which answers
   the name of a keyword as a string. ~|

:keyword
  |~~ The kind of a keyword, `:k`, a name that stands for itself. It is
      apart from the string so that no name is read by its look: every
      key of a map is a keyword, `/k` reads a field by it, and a keyword
      names a binding of the scope; `keyword` makes one of a string and
      a string of one. A bare keyword is a name that begins with a letter
      of any script, `_` or `@` and goes on with letters, digits, `_` and
      `-`; a slash joins names into a path, `:domain/user`;
      any other text is written in quotes, `:"foo bar"`, and the quotes
      around a bare name change nothing. ::link~(::map | doc) ::link~(::binding | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:keyword/isAKeyword
  |~~ `:k` is a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:k | type | eq ::keyword)

:keyword/buildsAKeyword
  |~~ The constructor of a keyword answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::keyword:k | eq :k)

:keyword/takesThreeForms
  |~~ A name of any script, a name with a path and a quoted name are keywords alike. ~~|
  ::law~([:данные :domain/user :"$ref"] | map ~keyword | eq ["данные" "domain/user" "$ref"])

:keyword/quotesABareName
  |~~ A bare name quoted is the same keyword. ~~|
  ::law~(:"foo" | eq :foo)

:keyword/construct
  |~~ Reads a keyword as itself. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/PayloadNotKeywordError
  |~~ `::keyword` reads its payload as the keyword it is; a string
      becomes one by `keyword`.
  ~~|

:keyword/PayloadNotKeywordError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string payload is refused; `keyword` turns a string into one. ~~|
  ::law~(::keyword"k" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::keyword/PayloadNotKeywordError ::string])

:keyword/gt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes after the keyword the
      call gives, `:b | gt :a`. Keywords compare by their names as they
      are spelled, letter by letter, so a capital comes before a small
      letter, `:a | gt :B` answering `true`. A keyword compares with a
      keyword alone: a string given is refused under the value, so text
      read from JSON, which arrives as a string, is turned by `keyword`
      first, `"b" | keyword | gt :a`, or compared with a string. `gte`
      answers whether the subject comes after the value or is the same
      keyword, `lt` and `lte` the reverse.
      ::link~(::keyword/gte | doc) ::link~(::keyword/lt | doc)
      ::link~(::keyword/lte | doc) ::link~(::string/keyword | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/gt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/gt/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | gt :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/gt/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | gt :a`; a string is
      turned by `keyword` first, `:b | gt ("a" | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/gt/ordersByName
  |~~ `:b` comes after `:a`. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gt :a)

:keyword/gt/putsACapitalFirst
  |~~ A capital comes before a small letter. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | gt :B)

:keyword/gt/keepsOneKeywordApartFromItself
  |~~ A keyword does not come after itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | gt :a | not)

:keyword/gt/comparesTextTurnedByKeyword
  |~~ Text turned by `keyword` compares with a keyword. ~~|
  ::law~("b" | keyword | gt :a)

:keyword/gt/value/NotKeywordError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | gt "a" !| type | eq ::keyword/gt/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/gte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes after the keyword the
      call gives or is the same keyword, `:b | gte :b`. It compares as
      `gt` does, keywords with keywords.
      ::link~(::keyword/gt | doc) ::link~(::keyword/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/gte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value or is the same
      keyword, `false` otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/gte/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | gte :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/gte/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | gte :a`; a string is
      turned by `keyword` first, `:b | gte ("a" | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/gte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A keyword comes after or is itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gte :b)

:keyword/gte/ordersByName
  |~~ `:b` comes after `:a`. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gte :a)

:keyword/gte/value/NotKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | gte 1 !| type | eq ::keyword/gte/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/lt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes before the keyword the
      call gives, `:a | lt :b`. It compares as `gt` does, keywords with
      keywords, a capital before a small letter, `:B | lt :a`.
      ::link~(::keyword/gt | doc) ::link~(::keyword/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/lt/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/lt/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | lt :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/lt/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | lt :a`; a string is
      turned by `keyword` first, `:b | lt ("a" | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/lt/ordersByName
  |~~ `:a` comes before `:b`. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lt :b)

:keyword/lt/putsACapitalFirst
  |~~ A capital comes before a small letter. ~~|
  ::law~(:B | lt :a)

:keyword/lt/keepsOneKeywordApartFromItself
  |~~ A keyword does not come before itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lt :a | not)

:keyword/lt/value/NotKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | lt 1 !| type | eq ::keyword/lt/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/lte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject, a keyword, comes before the keyword the
      call gives or is the same keyword, `:a | lte :a`. It compares as
      `gt` does, keywords with keywords.
      ::link~(::keyword/lt | doc) ::link~(::keyword/gte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/lte/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value or is the same
      keyword, `false` otherwise.
  ~~|
  ::boolean

:keyword/lte/value
  |~~ The keyword the subject is compared with, computed against the
      subject, `:b | lte :a`.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:keyword/lte/value/NotKeywordError
  |~~ The value compared is a keyword, `:b | lte :a`; a string is
      turned by `keyword` first, `:b | lte ("a" | keyword)`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword)

:keyword/lte/holdsOfItself
  |~~ A keyword comes before or is itself. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lte :a)

:keyword/lte/ordersByName
  |~~ `:a` comes before `:b`. ~~|
  ::law~(:a | lte :b)

:keyword/lte/value/NotKeywordError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no keyword to compare with. ~~|
  ::law~(:b | lte 1 !| type | eq ::keyword/lte/value/NotKeywordError)

:keyword/keyword
  |~~ Answers the name of the keyword as a string, `:foo | keyword`
      answering `"foo"`: the reverse of `keyword` on a string, which
      turns text into a keyword, so the two turn a name back and forth,
      `"foo" | keyword | keyword` answering `"foo"`. A quoted name and a
      name with a path answer their text as written, `:"foo bar"` the
      string `"foo bar"` and `:domain/user` the string `"domain/user"`.
      A value that is neither a keyword nor a string answers no
      `keyword`, which the refusal of the call names with the kinds that
      do.
      ::link~(::string/keyword | doc) ::link~(::keyword | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:keyword/keyword/answer
  |~~ The name of the keyword, a string. ~~|
  ::string

:keyword/keyword/answersTheName
  |~~ A keyword answers its name as a string. ~~|
  ::law~(:foo | keyword | eq "foo")

:keyword/keyword/keepsASpace
  |~~ A name with a space keeps it. ~~|
  ::law~(:"foo bar" | keyword | eq "foo bar")

:keyword/keyword/keepsAPath
  |~~ A name with a path answers it whole. ~~|
  ::law~(:domain/user | keyword | eq "domain/user")

:keyword/keyword/turnsATextBack
  |~~ A string turned into a keyword turns back into the string. ~~|
  ::law~("foo" | keyword | keyword | eq "foo")

:keyword/keyword/namesTheKindsThatAnswerIt
  |~~ A number answers no `keyword`, and the refusal names the kinds that do. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | keyword !| /addresses | eq #[::keyword/keyword ::string/keyword])
